# SYNTHETIC fixture. Not a captured sweep, and it is the one shape no captured
# sweep can be: a run that MEASURED a metric and recorded nothing at all about
# availability. Every real sweep runs the whole metric set against an endpoint
# and records a measurement or a decline for each, so the only way an endpoint's
# newest run holds no availability fact of either kind is a store whose runs
# were emitted at different metric revisions, which is the case
# web/queries/index.rq's own header designs for: "a store holding two runs at
# different metric revisions has two different metric sets in it at once".
#
# It exists for the index's final group. That group holds every endpoint whose
# newest run recorded no availability verdict, and the two ways to be in it are
# opposite claims: a run that DECLINED availability said so, and a run that
# recorded nothing about it said nothing. run-prober-failed.nq is the first,
# and this file is the second, so loading the two together puts one of each in
# one group and the group's sentence has to be true of both.
#
# Derived from run-classes-absent.nq by dropping its availability measurement
# and rewriting the endpoint and the instant, so what is left is one metric
# measured and no availability fact in either direction. No section terminator,
# for the same reason run-classes-absent.nq has none: this is not current
# emitter output, and a fixture claiming a terminator it was not emitted with
# would be claiming provenance it does not have.
#
# The instant is later than run-prober-failed.nq's 2026-08-23T02:00:00Z on
# purpose, so this run is the newest activity in the store the two build, and no
# terminator means no sw:emission, which is what keeps both endpoints' rows
# unqualified: EndpointMeasurements.newer_run_did_not_reach_this_endpoint
# requires newest_emission to be bound.
#
# 10 quads, one run graph (urn:sparqlwatch:run:2026-08-23T08:00:00Z), one
# endpoint, one dqv:QualityMeasurement (sw:metric:classes, "absent"), no
# sw:NotMeasured and no sw:ContentSample.
